---
/**
 * The About page. It was the Maker page until 30 August 2026.
 *
 * The client's Part 6 answer changed the page's direction: no personal
 * profile, no name, no portrait, no career history at this stage. Structure &
 * Style is presented as a company, in "we", and this page explains what the
 * company does, the standard it works to, and what a client can expect. The
 * personal story may return later as the business develops; nothing here
 * forecloses that.
 *
 * Two constraints survive from the maker version:
 *
 * Section 10 of the tone protocol is still an absolute factual limit: no crew
 * nouns, no workshop, no manufacturing claimed as ours. Company voice does not
 * license a single plural that implies staff. The protocol's mandate to state
 * the one-person fact plainly on this page was struck on 30 Aug 2026 (defect
 * register item 21, closed: the client's Q60 company framing is the settled
 * permanent direction, accepted by Denis on the scaling logic). What this page
 * says is the accountability fact that survives both rules and any future
 * hire: the person who measures is the person who draws and fits.
 *
 * No portrait appears. Q56 was withdrawn with the rest of Part 6.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Wrap from "../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import Display from "../components/ds/Display.astro";
import Kicker from "../components/ds/Kicker.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Plate from "../components/ds/Plate.astro";
import EnquireBand from "../components/ds/EnquireBand.astro";
import MonoLabel from "../components/ds/MonoLabel.astro";
import Link from "../components/ds/Link.astro";
import Value from "../components/Value.astro";

import { BUSINESS } from "../data/site";
import { BAND, CONTACT } from "../data/site-content";
import { indexable } from "../data/page-gates";

const isIndexable = indexable("/about/");

/**
 * EVERY SECTION OF THIS PAGE IS KASPAR'S OWN COPY from 6 September 2026. He
 * went down the page in the preview, screenshotted each section and typed its
 * replacement underneath, between 15:09 and 15:18. What follows in the markup
 * is that text, with his em dashes written as commas and nothing else changed.
 *
 * Three things went with the rewrite, and each is recorded where it stood:
 * the Q06, Q29 and Q35 values this page rendered, replaced by his later
 * sentences on the same facts; the accountability line ("the person who
 * measures your room is the person who draws it and fits it"), replaced by
 * the company framing he asked for on 30 Aug and wrote out himself here; and
 * the seven conduct practices from tone protocol section 11, which were
 * rendered verbatim as a list and are now three paragraphs of his own prose
 * covering the same ground. The seven stay in the protocol; the page carries
 * his version of them.
 */
---

<Base
  title="About Structure & Style"
  description="What Structure & Style does, the standard the work is held to, and what to expect from a commission, from the first visit through to aftercare."
  noindex={!isIndexable}
>
  <Wrap style="padding-top:var(--hero-y);padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <Kicker scribe>About</Kicker>
    <Display level={1} as="h1" style="margin:var(--space-12) 0 var(--space-13)">
      The standard we work to
    </Display>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);font-size:var(--type-lede);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      We design, measure and deliver bespoke fitted furniture for London homes. From the first
      survey through to installation and aftercare, each project is carefully planned and managed
      from start to finish.
    </p>

    {/* Added 2 Sep 2026. This page carried the standard, the guarantee and the
        seven conduct facts, and not one photograph, which protocol section 11
        calls the strongest proof we have and puts before any claim: a site in
        this market with no picture of finished work is not credible whatever
        it says. It was the only page under review making the full set of
        claims and the only one with nothing to look at.

        IT SILENTLY BECAME A DIFFERENT PHOTOGRAPH ONCE, 4 Sep 2026, caught
        5 Sep, and the mechanism is worth keeping even though the picture has
        since changed again. This page used to hold a filename rather than a
        photograph: fitted-wardrobes-02.webp, a slot in the gallery's own
        numbered set. When commit c594719 rebuilt that set from four frames to
        eight, the numbering shifted, slot 02 became a different room, and this
        page swapped its picture without a line of it being edited. The caption
        went on saying "loft room" about a photograph that was not one.

        Two lessons. A shared numbered set is not a stable address, so a page
        outside that set must never point into it by index. And a comment
        describing a photograph is a test: when the words beside the code stop
        matching what the code renders, something moved.

        THE PHOTOGRAPH, chosen by Denis on 5 Sep 2026. A whole room rather than
        a single piece, and that is the argument the lede above makes: lit
        alcove shelving and built-in cupboards down the left, a second run of
        shelving and cupboards past the fireplace, and the kitchen through the
        opening, all in one room. A page that says one commission runs from the
        first visit to aftercare and does not change hands should show the
        several things one commission covers, not one wardrobe.

        It also happens to be the best-lit frame in the library, from the
        professional set in the 31 August drop that Denis confirmed as Kaspar's
        work. It renders elsewhere as bespoke-kitchens-08, which is a duplicate
        across two pages, and it is a deliberate one: that page is noindexed
        wave two and carries eight other frames.

        Its own asset at /images/about/, not a gallery slot, so the renumbering
        above cannot happen to it. Cut from the 1643 x 1095 export: the blank
        wall and door frame come off the right edge, a little ceiling off the
        top, and the band lands at 1.70 with the armchairs framing the bottom
        rather than filling it. No upscaling, so 1552 is what the source
        actually holds, and the plate declares that ratio so nothing is cropped
        twice. */}
    <div style="margin-top:var(--plate-y)">
      <Plate
        photo="/images/about/living-room-alcoves-kitchen.webp"
        alt={`Lit alcove shelving and built-in cupboards either side of a fireplace, opening through to a bespoke kitchen, completed installation by ${BUSINESS.legalName}`}
        width={1552}
        height={912}
        subject="Plate 01 · Alcove shelving and bespoke kitchen"
        detail="Completed"
        ratio="1552 / 912"
        eager
        plane="left"
      />
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style="padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <SectionHead meta="Our approach" level={4}>How a commission runs</SectionHead>
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
      Each project follows a clear process, from the initial consultation and survey through to
      design, preparation, installation and aftercare.
    </p>
    {/* The four names are the ones he fixed the same day for the homepage and
        /process/. This line said "visit, survey and drawings" until then. */}
    <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-8);color:var(--text-quiet)">
      Four stages, the same four every time: visit and survey, quotation and drawings, preparation,
      fitting.
      <Link href="/process/" variant="nav" style="margin-left:var(--space-5)">The full process, stage by stage</Link>
    </p>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="In plain terms" level={4}>The standard</SectionHead>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        Every project carries our name. It is set out, levelled, plumb, fitted and finished with the
        same care in the details you see and the ones you don't. It should look right, feel right
        and be built to last.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="Accountability" level={4}>Who does the work</SectionHead>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        Structure &amp; Style manages each project from initial survey and design through to
        installation and aftercare, taking responsibility for the work and the finished result
        throughout.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="Design" level={4}>Before anything is ordered</SectionHead>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        Every project begins with a measured survey and a clear, itemised quotation. Once the
        project is agreed, the design is developed as required, with drawings and elevations
        provided for review before anything is ordered or put into production. Up to two rounds of
        revisions are included.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="Materials" level={4}>Materials, chosen for the project</SectionHead>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        Materials and finishes are selected around the design, how the furniture will be used and
        the look you want to achieve. From painted and decorative boards to natural wood veneers,
        each specification is chosen for the project rather than applied as a standard formula.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
        <Link href="/materials/" variant="nav" chamfer>The range, the finishes and the reasoning</Link>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="In your home" level={4}>What to expect while we work</SectionHead>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        We understand that most of our work takes place in occupied homes, so protecting the
        property and keeping disruption to a minimum is part of every project.
      </p>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-8)">
        Floors and surrounding areas are protected before work begins, with dust extraction used
        throughout cutting and installation. Where possible, cutting is carried out outside or in a
        designated work area.
      </p>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-8)">
        The workspace is kept organised and tidied at the end of each day, with waste and off-cuts
        removed on completion. Where a property is managed, we coordinate access, working hours,
        deliveries and any required documentation with the building or managing agent before work
        begins.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="Afterwards" level={4}>Once the work is finished</SectionHead>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        Our work is covered for 2 years on installation and workmanship and 10 years on the
        structure and joinery, with manufacturer warranties applying to hinges, runners and other
        moving hardware.
      </p>
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure);margin-top:var(--space-8)">
        If doors or other moving parts require adjustment within the 2-year aftercare period, we
        come back and take care of them. Care guidance for the materials and finishes used is
        provided on completion.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-16)">
      <SectionHead meta="Where we work" level={4}>Service area</SectionHead>
      {/* His sentence, 6 September 2026: "We work across London, including
          ...". It rendered Q03 ("Serving homes across London") until the
          verification run read the two side by side. Q03 stays as it is for
          the enquiry band, where a sentence in the first person would read
          wrong in mono caps; here the district list is still the one source. */}
      <p style="max-width:var(--measure)">
        We work across London, including {BUSINESS.areaServed.slice(0, -1).join(", ")} and{" "}
        {BUSINESS.areaServed.slice(-1)}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <EnquireBand heading="If you have a room in mind, start here." chamfer>
    {BAND.body}
    <Fragment slot="facts">
      {BAND.facts.map((q) => (
        <MonoLabel size="md" tone="paper-soft" style="letter-spacing:.1em">
          <Value q={q} />
        </MonoLabel>
      ))}
    </Fragment>
  </EnquireBand>
</Base>
